==Post-show career==
[[Liam Charles]] was chosen as a co-presenter for the revamped professional version of ''Great British Bake Off'', ''[[Bake Off: The Professionals]]'',<ref>{{cite news |url=http://metro.co.uk/2018/02/07/liam-charles-host-great-british-bake-off-professionals-spin-off-show-channel-4-7294552/ |title= Liam Charles to host Great British Bake Off: The Professionals spin-off show on Channel 4 |first=Adam|last= Bloodworth|date= 7 February 2018 |work=Metro}}</ref> and in 2019 returned as co-judge for ''[[Junior Bake Off]]''.  He has written two recipe books on baking; ''Cheeky Treats: 70 Brilliant Bakes and Cakes'',<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2018/jul/11/liam-charles-cake-best-way-to-make-people-happy|title= Bake Off's Liam Charles: 'Cake is the best way to make people happy' |first=Zoe |last=Williams |date=11 July 2018|work=The Guardian }}</ref> and '' Second Helpings''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://books.telegraph.co.uk/Product/Liam-Charles/Liam-Charles-Second-Helpings--70-wicked-recipes-that-will/23626339 |title= Liam Charles Second Helpings : 70 wicked recipes that will leave you wanting more Hardback by Liam Charles |work=The Telegraph}}</ref>

[[Sophie Faldo]] runs her own business, Sophie Faldo Cakes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bustle.com/p/where-are-all-the-past-bake-off-winners-now-these-star-bakers-have-been-busy-since-winning-the-series-19276029 |title=Where Are All The Past 'Bake Off' Winners Now? These Star Bakers Have Been Busy Since Winning The Series |first= Sophie|last= McEvoy|date=29 October 2019|work=Bustle }}</ref> She has also appeared in an episode of a travelogue TV series ''Travels with a Goat''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/tv/2019/01/14/travels-goat-review-grime-caked-travelogue-confronting-ethics/ |title=Travels with a Goat, review - a grime-caked travelogue confronting the ethics of eating meat |work=The Telegraph|date= 14 January 2019|first=Ed|last=Power}}</ref>

Several bakers participated in ''The Great Christmas Bake Off'' and ''The Great New Year's Bake Off'' in the following years. In 2018, Flo Atkins and Liam Charles participated in ''The Great Christmas Bake Off'' while Steven Carter-Bailey participated in ''The Great New Year's Bake Off'' and won the special's star baker title. In 2019, Tom Hetherington and Yan Tsou participated in ''The Great Christmas Bake Off''. In 2020, James Hillery participated in ''The Great Christmas Bake Off''. In 2023, Sophie Faldo participated in ''The Great Christmas Bake Off'' and won the special's star baker title.

== Controversies ==
On 31 October 2017, judge [[Prue Leith]] accidentally revealed [[Sophie Faldo]] as the winner of the series  on [[Twitter]] twelve hours before the finale was due to air. This caused uproar among many fans of the show. She quickly deleted the tweet and apologised to the fans who saw the tweet.<ref>{{cite news |last= McCormack |first=Kirsty |date= 31 October 2017 |title= Great British Bake Off 2017 winner REVEALED by judge Prue Leith: 'I'm mortified' |url= http://www.express.co.uk/showbiz/tv-radio/873509/Great-British-Bake-Off-2017-winner-judge-Prue-Leith-spoiler-Twitter |work= [[Daily Express]] |access-date= 31 October 2017}}</ref>

== Ratings ==
The first episode to be broadcast on Channel 4 received an overnight viewing figure of 6.5 million, which is the channel's highest viewing figure since the opening ceremony for the [[2012 Summer Paralympics|2012 Paralympics]], but down from the 10 million obtained in the previous series on BBC One.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2017/aug/30/great-british-bake-off-proves-ratings-winner-for-channel-4 |title=Great British Bake Off proves ratings winner for Channel 4 |first=Graham |last=Ruddick |date= 30 August 2017 |work=The Guardian }}</ref>  The overnight viewing figure for the final was 7.7 million (8.9 million at its peak), lower than the 14 million for the equivalent episode on BBC One in 2016.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/stayingin/tvfilm/great-british-bake-off-2017-final-brings-in-channel-4-s-biggest-audience-for-six-years-despite-gbbo-a3673481.html |title=Great British Bake Off final brings in Channel 4's biggest audience for six years despite judge Prue Leith's accidental early reveal |first= Aimée |last=Grant Cumberbatch  |work= The Evening Standard |date=1 November 2017}}</ref>

Official episode viewing figures are from [[BARB]]. Figures are total counts including HD and Channel 4 +1.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.barb.co.uk/whats-new/weekly-top-30?_s=4|title=Weekly Top 30 Programmes|publisher=[[Broadcasters' Audience Research Board]]|access-date=1 September 2017}}</ref>

